const slug = "amazon-ending-support-13-kindles-2026";
const title = "Amazon Ends Support for 13 Kindles: Save Your eBook Library";
const description = "Amazon ends support for 13 Kindle models in 2026. See affected devices and convert your library to EPUB before you lose access.";
const pubDate = "2026-07-07";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonicalUrl={`https://converter-epub.com/blog/${slug}/`} ogType="article" datePublished={pubDate}>
  <main class="blog-post">
    <article itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article">
      <meta itemprop="headline" content={title} />
      <meta itemprop="description" content={description} />
      <meta itemprop="datePublished" content="2026-07-07" />
      <span itemprop="author" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Person">
        <span itemprop="name">Jason Mai</span>
      </span>

      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-07-07">Published July 7, 2026</time></p>

      <div set:html={`
<p>Amazon has announced it will end cloud and content support for 13 older Kindle models, cutting off WhisperSync syncing, dictionary lookups, Wikipedia search, and direct Kindle Store access. The move follows the May 2026 cutoff of store access for pre-2012 Kindles and expands the list of affected devices significantly.</p> <p>The change affects Kindles released between 2011 and 2015, including multiple generations of the Kindle Touch, Kindle Paperwhite, and Kindle Fire tablet lines. If you own an older Kindle, your device will still function as a reading device for books already on it, but the cloud features that many readers rely on will stop working.</p> <p>This article explains exactly which models are affected, what you lose, and — most importantly — how to download your Kindle library and convert it to EPUB so your books remain readable on any device, now and in the future.</p> <h2>Which Kindle Models Are Affected</h2> <p>Amazon has not published a single official list of the 13 models, but multiple news sources (BBC, Forbes, Mashable, PCMag, Good e-Reader) have confirmed the affected devices through Amazon's internal support documentation and retailer communications. The models fall into three groups:</p> <table> <tr><th>Group</th><th>Models</th><th>Release Year</th></tr> <tr><td>Kindle Touch Series</td><td>Kindle Touch (4th gen), Kindle Touch 3G</td><td>2011</td></tr> <tr><td>Kindle Paperwhite 1st Gen</td><td>Kindle Paperwhite (1st gen)</td><td>2012</td></tr> <tr><td>Kindle Fire Tablets</td><td>Kindle Fire HD (1st gen), Kindle Fire HDX 7&Prime;, Kindle Fire HDX 8.9&Prime;, Kindle Fire (2nd gen), Kindle Fire HD (2nd gen)</td><td>2012-2014</td></tr> <tr><td>Older E-Readers</td><td>Kindle 4 (no keyboard), Kindle 5, Kindle DX</td><td>2010-2011</td></tr> </table> <p>If you own any model older than the 7th-generation Kindle (2014), there is a strong chance it is on the list. The 7th-generation Kindle (Kindle 7, 2014) and the Kindle Paperwhite 2nd generation and newer are not affected — for now.</p> <h2>What Actually Stops Working</h2> <p>Amazon's end-of-support affects cloud-connected features, not local reading. Here is what changes:</p> <table> <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Status After Support Ends</th></tr> <tr><td>Reading books already on the device</td><td>Still works</td></tr> <tr><td>Kindle Store purchases and downloads</td><td>Stops working</td></tr> <tr><td>WhisperSync (sync reading position across devices)</td><td>Stops working</td></tr> <tr><td>Dictionary and Wikipedia lookup</td><td>Stops working</td></tr> <tr><td>Cloud storage access to Amazon archive</td><td>Stops working</td></tr> <tr><td>Download &amp; Transfer via USB (from Amazon website)</td><td>Still works (for now)</td></tr> <tr><td>Sideloading via Calibre or Send to Kindle</td><td>Still works (DRM-free books only)</td></tr> <tr><td>Reading books transferred via USB</td><td>Still works</td></tr> </table> <p>The most consequential loss is <strong>WhisperSync</strong>. If you read on multiple devices (a Kindle at home and the Kindle app on your phone on the go), syncing between them will break. Your reading position will be stored locally only, and you will need to manually find your place when switching devices.</p> <p>Loss of direct Kindle Store access means you can no longer buy new books on the affected device itself. You can still purchase books from Amazon on a computer or phone and transfer them to the Kindle via USB, as long as the book's format is compatible with the device's firmware.</p> <h2>How to Save Your Kindle Library Before Support Ends</h2> <p>The most important step you can take right now is to <strong>download every Kindle book you own</strong> and convert it to a portable format like EPUB. Here is the step-by-step process:</p> <h3>Step 1: Download Your Kindle Books from Amazon</h3> <p>Amazon still allows you to download purchased Kindle books through the Download &amp; Transfer via USB feature on the Amazon website:</p> <ol> <li>Go to <strong>Amazon.com &gt; Account &amp; Lists &gt; Content &amp; Devices</strong></li> <li>Select the <strong>Books</strong> tab to see all your Kindle purchases</li> <li>Click the <strong>Download &amp; Transfer via USB</strong> button next to each book</li> <li>Download the <code>.azw3</code> file to your computer</li> </ol> <p>Repeat this for every book in your library. This is the one step that has a time limit — once Amazon fully cuts off the affected models, the Download &amp; Transfer option may also disappear from your account for books purchased on those devices.</p> <h3>Step 2: Remove DRM from Your Kindle Files</h3> <p>The downloaded <code>.azw3</code> files still have Amazon's DRM encryption. To convert them to EPUB, you first need to remove the DRM. The standard tool for this is <strong>Calibre</strong> with the DeDRM plugin installed:</p> <ol> <li><strong>Install Calibre</strong> from <code>calibre-ebook.com</code> if you do not have it already</li> <li><strong>Install the DeDRM plugin</strong> by downloading the latest DeDRM tools from the Apprentice Alf GitHub repository</li> <li><strong>Add your Kindle serial number</strong> to the DeDRM plugin preferences (you can find the serial in Settings &gt; Device Info on your Kindle)</li> <li><strong>Add the downloaded <code>.azw3</code> files</strong> to Calibre &mdash; the plugin will automatically strip the DRM during import</li> </ol> <p>You only need to do this setup once. After the plugin is configured, every Kindle book you drag into Calibre will have its DRM removed automatically.</p> <h3>Step 3: Convert Your Books to EPUB</h3> <p>Once the books are in Calibre with DRM removed, converting them to EPUB is straightforward:</p> <ol> <li>Select all the books in Calibre (Ctrl+A or Cmd+A)</li> <li>Right-click and choose <strong>Convert Books &gt; Convert Individually</strong></li> <li>Set the output format to <strong>EPUB</strong> in the top-right dropdown</li> <li>Click <strong>OK</strong> to start the batch conversion</li> </ol> <p>Calibre will process each book in sequence. For a library of 200 books, expect the full conversion to take 5-10 minutes. The resulting EPUB files are fully portable and can be read on any EPUB-compatible e-reader: Kobo, PocketBook, Boox, or the Kindle app on a phone.</p> <h3>Step 4: Transfer Your EPUBs to Your New Reader</h3> <p>With your library in EPUB format, you are no longer tied to Amazon's ecosystem. Transfer the EPUB files to your preferred device using any of these methods:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Kobo:</strong> Connect via USB and drag EPUB files to the main folder, or use the built-in OverDrive sync</li> <li><strong>PocketBook:</strong> Use PocketBook Cloud or USB transfer &mdash; EPUB is the native format</li> <li><strong>Boox (Android):</strong> Transfer via Google Drive, Dropbox, or USB; read with any EPUB reader app</li> <li><strong>Calibre Content Server:</strong> Host your library on your home network and access books over Wi-Fi</li> </ul> <p>If you plan to keep using your old Kindle as a reading device (without cloud features), you can convert EPUB back to AZW3 using <a href="/blog/calibre-cli-batch-conversion/">Calibre CLI batch conversion</a> and transfer via USB. The device will still read local files.</p> <h2>Alternative: Use the Amazon Kindle for PC/Mac App</h2> <p>If the DeDRM process sounds technically involved, there is a simpler path for some books. Amazon's Kindle for PC and Kindle for Mac applications download books in a format that can be accessed from the local file system:</p> <ol> <li>Install Kindle for PC/Mac on your computer</li> <li>Download each book within the app</li> <li>Navigate to the local Kindle content folder (typically <code>My Kindle Content</code> on Windows)</li> <li>Copy the <code>.azw</code> files to a backup location</li> <li>Use Calibre to convert these files to EPUB</li> </ol> <p>This method works for books that have a PC/Mac reading option enabled by the publisher. Some titles are Kindle-device-only and will not appear in the desktop app's local folder.</p> <h2>What About Kindle Unlimited and Library Books?</h2> <p>Books borrowed through <strong>Kindle Unlimited</strong> or <strong>Kindle Owners' Lending Library</strong> cannot be downloaded and transferred. They are only readable while your subscription is active, and the DRM on KU books cannot be removed through the standard DeDRM process.</p> <p>The same applies to library books borrowed through OverDrive/Kindle integration. Once the lending period expires, the book is automatically returned to the library. If you have borrowed books you want to keep, check whether they are available as DRM-free EPUBs on Kobo (which supports OverDrive) or directly from the publisher.</p> <h2>What This Means for EPUB Readers</h2> <p>Amazon's expanding end-of-support list is a reminder that a Kindle library is not a permanent library. Every Kindle book you own is tied to Amazon's servers for DRM authentication, format compatibility, and cloud-based features. When support ends for a device, your access to those features — and potentially to the books themselves — is at risk.</p> <p>The EPUB format, by contrast, is device-independent and format-stable. An EPUB file created today will open on any EPUB-compatible reader for decades. No cloud dependency. No firmware update required. No DRM server that can go offline.</p> <p>If you own a Kindle that is approaching end-of-support, the time to act is now. Download your library, remove the DRM, and convert everything to EPUB. Once your books are in EPUB format, you can read them on any e-reader, on any phone, or on any computer — regardless of what Amazon decides to do next.</p> <section class="faq" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage"> <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Which Kindle models are affected by the 2026 support cutoff?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Amazon is ending support for 13 models including the Kindle Touch (4th gen), Kindle Touch 3G, 1st-generation Kindle Paperwhite, Kindle Fire HD (1st and 2nd gen), Kindle Fire HDX, Kindle 4, Kindle 5, and Kindle DX. If your Kindle was released before 2014, it is likely affected.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Can I still read books on my Kindle after support ends?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Yes. Books that are already downloaded to your Kindle will continue to be readable. You lose cloud features like WhisperSync, dictionary lookup, and Kindle Store access, but the device itself still works as a local reading device.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">How do I download my Kindle books to my computer?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Go to Amazon.com &gt; Account &amp; Lists &gt; Content &amp; Devices. Select the Books tab, then click Download &amp; Transfer via USB next to each book. This downloads the <code>.azw3</code> file to your computer for backup and conversion.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">How do I convert my Kindle books to EPUB?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Use Calibre with the DeDRM plugin. Install Calibre, add your Kindle serial number to the DeDRM plugin, import your downloaded <code>.azw3</code> files (DRM is stripped automatically), then select all books and convert to EPUB. The resulting files are fully portable.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Does the new Amazon DRM-free ebook program help affected device owners?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Partially. In July 2026, Amazon began showing DRM-free badges on some Kindle book product pages and enabling EPUB downloads for select titles. For books you already own, however, past purchases still use the old DRM system. The new DRM-free program mainly benefits future purchases from participating publishers.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Can I transfer EPUB files to my affected Kindle?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Kindles do not natively read EPUB files. Use Calibre to convert EPUB to AZW3 or MOBI first, then transfer via USB with Calibre's device driver or manually copy the files to the Kindle's documents folder. Send to Kindle also works for DRM-free MOBI and AZW files.</p> </div> </div>
      `}/>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
